1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,500
Let me start with the result.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:07,067
Last night, I lost to an eight-year-old.

3
00:00:07,167 --> 00:00:09,733
Over dinner. Twice.

4
00:00:10,433 --> 00:00:15,333
My kid was staring at a friend's birthday invitation, and then asked:

5
00:00:15,433 --> 00:00:19,133
"Dad, it says my friend lives in an officetel."

6
00:00:19,233 --> 00:00:23,333
"What's the difference between an apartment, a villa and an officetel?"

7
00:00:24,033 --> 00:00:29,767
"Um... an apartment is a bit bigger, and a bit... pricier."

8
00:00:29,867 --> 00:00:34,167
Asked about the names of homes. Answered with the price of homes.

9
00:00:34,267 --> 00:00:36,333
"Then how much is our house?"

10
00:00:36,433 --> 00:00:37,167
Strike one.

11
00:00:37,867 --> 00:00:41,600
"Uh... that's... a secret. Eat your dinner."

12
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:43,900
I hid behind my spoon.

13
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:44,900
Strike two.

14
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,767
"Dad, it's okay to say you don't know."

15
00:00:47,867 --> 00:00:49,900
...Yes. I didn't know.

16
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:55,367
That night, in a dark room, I quietly turned on my phone.

17
00:00:55,467 --> 00:01:01,633
The creature known as Dad, when cornered, searches the internet in secret at night.

18
00:01:01,733 --> 00:01:05,700
I typed this into the search bar: "villa meaning."

19
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:09,133
And I lost again, on the very first line.

20
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:14,767
Korea's Building Act lists every type of housing, one by one.

21
00:01:14,867 --> 00:01:20,233
Apartment. Row house. Multi-household house. Multi-family house.

22
00:01:20,333 --> 00:01:22,933
"Villa" isn't there.

23
00:01:23,033 --> 00:01:26,633
We say it every day, and the law doesn't mention it once.

24
00:01:26,733 --> 00:01:30,567
So what exactly have we been calling a villa?

25
00:01:30,667 --> 00:01:34,700
Let's trace who gave each of these three names.

26
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:44,467
First, the apartment. In 1962, a cluster of six-storey buildings went up all at once in Mapo, Seoul.

27
00:01:44,567 --> 00:01:49,733
Mapo Apartments, regarded as Korea's first apartment complex.

28
00:01:49,833 --> 00:01:52,900
Today the law defines an apartment in a single line.

29
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,133
Five or more floors of homes: that's an apartment.

30
00:01:57,233 --> 00:02:02,467
Not the size. Not the price. Not the brand. The number of floors.

31
00:02:02,567 --> 00:02:05,567
So my first answer was already wrong, right here.

32
00:02:06,267 --> 00:02:15,333
And up to four floors? The law calls those row houses and multi-household houses.

33
00:02:15,433 --> 00:02:20,333
They do sound like forms at the district office.

34
00:02:20,433 --> 00:02:28,333
But there's a suspicious building in every neighborhood. Count it and it's clearly five floors. Yet it's a villa.

35
00:02:28,433 --> 00:02:33,933
The secret is the ground floor: a car park with nothing but pillars.

36
00:02:34,033 --> 00:02:38,133
The law doesn't count that car park as a floor.

37
00:02:38,233 --> 00:02:43,233
Five floors to the eye, four on paper. So it's not an apartment.

38
00:02:43,333 --> 00:02:46,800
Which is a little unfair to the car park, honestly.

39
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:50,300
Then who came up with the name "villa"?

40
00:02:50,400 --> 00:02:57,167
Villa is Latin. It was the country house that rich ancient Romans kept outside the city.

41
00:02:57,867 --> 00:03:03,200
The name is said to have spread in Korea around the 1980s.

42
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:08,100
"Villas for sale" simply sounded a lot better than "multi-household houses for sale."

43
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:13,800
Before that, the trendy name was "mansion." As in, a great big manor.

44
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:19,633
Going by the names alone, everyone in Korea lives in either a country estate or a manor.

45
00:03:20,333 --> 00:03:24,533
Last, the officetel. It's "office" and "hotel" stuck together.

46
00:03:24,633 --> 00:03:29,200
It looks like English, but it's a Korean coinage hardly used in English-speaking countries.

47
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:36,700
The first one is said to have opened in 1985 in Mapo, Seoul. Yes, Mapo again.

48
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:43,100
The key point: the law doesn't treat an officetel as a home. It's a business facility. An office.

49
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,500
An office where you've been given permission to eat and sleep.

50
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,033
My company never gave that permission, and yet somehow everyone does it anyway.

51
00:03:52,733 --> 00:03:54,133
So, to sum up:

52
00:03:54,233 --> 00:03:57,100
Apartments were defined by floor count,

53
00:03:57,200 --> 00:03:59,333
villas were built by advertising,

54
00:03:59,433 --> 00:04:02,133
and officetels were born in the office.

55
00:04:03,833 --> 00:04:07,067
The next evening, I called my kid over to the window.

56
00:04:07,167 --> 00:04:13,200
"Dad didn't really answer you yesterday, did he? Truth is, I didn't know either. So I looked it up."

57
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:17,633
"Stack homes five floors or higher and it's an apartment. Like that tall one over there."

58
00:04:17,733 --> 00:04:26,400
"Up to four floors, the real name is "multi-household house," which is way too hard. So people gave it a fancy nickname: villa."

59
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:32,867
"And an officetel is really an office building, made so people can sleep there too."

60
00:04:32,967 --> 00:04:38,267
"And when I said yesterday that apartments are the expensive ones, that was wrong."

61
00:04:38,367 --> 00:04:44,833
"These names sort buildings by how many floors they have. They don't sort the people who live in them."

62
00:04:45,533 --> 00:04:48,800
My kid stared out the window for a long while, then asked:

63
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:51,367
"Then how many floors is that building?"

64
00:04:51,467 --> 00:04:55,533
"Shall we count together? Skip the car park on the ground floor."

65
00:04:55,633 --> 00:04:58,400
"One, two, three, four."

66
00:04:58,500 --> 00:05:00,000
"It's a villa!"

67
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:05,633
And that's how, after two losses, I scraped one precious win.

68
00:05:05,733 --> 00:05:10,033
Once you know the names, your neighborhood looks a little different.

69
00:05:10,133 --> 00:05:15,900
On your way home today, if you spot a building with a car park on the ground floor, try counting.

70
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:18,967
And then, at dinner that very evening:

71
00:05:19,067 --> 00:05:23,933
"Dad, so what's the difference between a mart, a market and a department store?"

72
00:05:24,033 --> 00:05:25,333
"...Eat your dinner."

73
00:05:25,433 --> 00:05:27,233
"I am eating."

74
00:05:27,933 --> 00:05:33,000
See you next time. I've got some searching to do before then.
